= Cryptography — Technical =

== Primitive / protocol distinction ==
Primitives include encryption schemes, hashes, MACs, signatures, key-derivation functions and pseudorandom functions.

Protocols compose primitives to achieve higher-level properties.

Secure primitives do not guarantee secure composition.

== Security parameter ==
Modern cryptography indexes algorithms by security parameter λ.

A typical computational-security claim bounds adversarial advantage by a negligible function:

<pre>
Adv_A(λ) ≤ negl(λ)
</pre>

under specified assumptions.

== Indistinguishability ==
Encryption security can be formalized through games such as IND-CPA or IND-CCA, depending on adversarial capabilities.

The correct definition depends on the intended threat model.

== Assumptions ==
Security can depend on mathematical hardness assumptions, idealized models, trusted setup, random oracles, implementation assumptions, or combinations.

== Side channels ==
Timing, power, cache state, fault injection and metadata can leak information outside the abstract cryptographic model.

